{
  "teamId": 137,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 San Francisco Giants opened their season with a burst of electricity, fueled by timely hitting and a sense that this roster had something to prove. Wilmer Flores provided the first signature moment of the year, launching a three-run homer on Opening Day to set the tone, and Willy Adames followed days later with a walk-off single that had Oracle Park buzzing. For a brief stretch in late March and early April, the Giants looked like a team capable of contending in the NL West.\n\nAs spring gave way to summer, however, the cracks began to show. The bullpen — anchored at times precariously by Camilo Doval and Ryan Walker — became a recurring liability, surrendering leads in high-leverage moments throughout May and June. Walker was victimized multiple times, and Doval surrendered critical home runs that deflated what could have been momentum-building wins. Matt Chapman's grand slam in early May and his go-ahead home run on June 7th — a +0.901 WPA swing, the single biggest play of the Giants' season — were brilliant individual moments, but they couldn't paper over a roster that was grinding rather than soaring.\n\nThe mid-season stretch from July through August told a tale of a team treading water. Patrick Bailey's improbable inside-the-park home run in early July provided a jolt of joy, and Heliot Ramos emerged as a reliable presence in the outfield with clutch late-game hits. But the bullpen continued to surrender leads, and Randy Rodríguez gave up pivotal runs in August that further eroded any wild card hopes the Giants were clinging to.\n\nBy September, the Giants found themselves in a familiar and frustrating place — mathematically alive but practically eliminated from contention. Robbie Ray surrendering a Shohei Ohtani home run in mid-September and Ryan Walker's late-September collapse against St. Louis served as bookends to a bullpen season best described as maddeningly inconsistent. San Francisco finished 81-81, a mirror image of mediocrity — talented enough to win, brittle enough to lose — and missed the playoffs for another year, leaving fans to wonder what a more reliable late-game unit might have produced.",
